sections.s2_technical:
A spin lock is the simplest mutual-exclusion primitive: an atomic boolean or integer that a thread flips from free to held using compare-and-swap in a loop. If the CAS fails, the thread loops and tries again rather than going to sleep.

```java
AtomicBoolean locked = new AtomicBoolean(false);
void lock() {
    while (!locked.compareAndSet(false, true)) {  // spin until we flip false→true
        Thread.onSpinWait();                      // hint: this is a spin-wait
    }
}
void unlock() { locked.set(false); }
```

`Thread.onSpinWait()` does not change program logic; it is purely a performance hint to the hardware. On x86 it typically maps to the PAUSE instruction, which reduces power use during the spin, avoids a memory-order violation penalty when the loop exits, and improves throughput of a sibling hyperthread sharing the core. You place it inside the body of any spin loop.

Spinning helps when three things hold: the expected wait is very short (shorter than the cost of a context switch), there is a spare CPU core so spinning does not steal time from the thread that will release the lock, and contention is low. A classic example is a lock protecting a handful of instructions, where the holder releases almost immediately.

Spinning harms when the wait is long, because the thread burns a whole core doing nothing, raising CPU usage and power with zero progress. It is especially bad when cores are oversubscribed: if the spinning thread is scheduled on the same core as the thread holding the lock, the spinner prevents the holder from running to release the lock, which can be catastrophic. It also wastes energy on battery-powered or cloud-metered environments.

The pragmatic middle ground is adaptive or bounded spinning: spin a limited number of times, and if the lock is still not acquired, fall back to blocking (parking the thread). The JVM's own intrinsic locks and `java.util.concurrent` locks do exactly this — they spin briefly in the hope of a quick acquire, then park if that fails. This is why in application code you almost never write a raw spin lock; you use `ReentrantLock` or `synchronized`, which already blend short spinning with blocking.

```java
int spins = 0;
while (!locked.compareAndSet(false, true)) {
    if (++spins < 100) { Thread.onSpinWait(); }   // bounded spin
    else { LockSupport.parkNanos(1_000); spins = 0; }  // then back off / park
}
```

A senior nuance: spinning is a latency optimization, not a throughput optimization. It shaves microseconds off the critical path for ultra-low-latency systems like trading engines, but it costs CPU that could serve other requests. So I only introduce explicit spinning where latency is paramount and the wait is provably tiny; everywhere else I block.

